WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.760 --> 00:00:11.240
Fierce, sassy, va va voom.

00:00:11.320 --> 00:00:13.080
Finger-snapping, neck winding,

00:00:13.160 --> 00:00:14.640
bootylicious realness.

00:00:15.040 --> 00:00:16.840
Jam-packed with attitude.

00:00:17.040 --> 00:00:19.680
Strong, independent black woman.

00:00:20.560 --> 00:00:21.920
That's me right?

00:00:24.160 --> 00:00:25.600
Am I doing it right?

00:00:27.200 --> 00:00:30.640
I'm asking because I often feel
like I must be doing it wrong.

00:00:31.440 --> 00:00:35.240
I must be because it
seems so easy for those

00:00:35.320 --> 00:00:39.400
stoical, wise-cracking black women
I see on the TV screen.

00:00:40.040 --> 00:00:41.880
This is what's expected of me.

00:00:43.120 --> 00:00:44.880
What happens if you
don't fit the mould?

00:00:44.960 --> 00:00:48.320
What happens if you're made to feel
that by just being yourself,

00:00:48.400 --> 00:00:49.560
you're doing it wrong?

00:00:49.840 --> 00:00:52.760
What effect can this have
on your mental wellbeing?

00:00:53.160 --> 00:00:55.280
Depression was something that
happened to other people -

00:00:55.480 --> 00:00:56.160
not me.

00:00:56.240 --> 00:00:59.840
Ain't nobody got time for that. Too
busy being strong and independent -

00:00:59.920 --> 00:01:01.760
working twice as hard
as everyone else

00:01:02.040 --> 00:01:04.160
and trying to make good use
of the opportunities

00:01:04.240 --> 00:01:05.760
that now presented
themselves to me

00:01:05.840 --> 00:01:09.000
because of the grit and determination
of the strong black women

00:01:09.320 --> 00:01:11.200
who had paved the way before me.

00:01:13.120 --> 00:01:16.320
So I was shocked when depression
came for me in 2008.

00:01:16.920 --> 00:01:19.160
What was most shocking
was the realisation that

00:01:19.240 --> 00:01:23.080
it had been there for a very long
time waiting in the shadows.

00:01:23.160 --> 00:01:26.280
I felt like I'd failed
until I did some research

00:01:26.360 --> 00:01:28.000
and discovered that
this is more common

00:01:28.080 --> 00:01:29.520
than I've been led to believe.

00:01:30.360 --> 00:01:34.200
Stats from Gov.uk show that four
times as many black people

00:01:34.280 --> 00:01:37.520
were sectioned under the mental
health act in 2017 to 18

00:01:37.600 --> 00:01:39.960
than white people -
four times as many.

00:01:40.280 --> 00:01:44.560
According to the NHS, black British
women are more prone than white women

00:01:44.640 --> 00:01:46.560
to experience common mental disorders

00:01:46.640 --> 00:01:47.520
such as...

00:01:51.120 --> 00:01:53.440
And a recent
University of Cambridge study

00:01:53.520 --> 00:01:56.560
revealed that "Black women
aged between 16 and 34..."

00:02:00.080 --> 00:02:02.040
Something isn't adding up here.

00:02:03.160 --> 00:02:04.560
The data's saying one thing

00:02:04.640 --> 00:02:09.080
but social media, memes and the
entertainment industry in general

00:02:09.160 --> 00:02:12.480
are still pushing the strong,
independent stereotype.

00:02:12.720 --> 00:02:15.160
After I was diagnosed with depression

00:02:15.480 --> 00:02:18.720
I suddenly became aware
that I didn't see the world

00:02:18.800 --> 00:02:20.560
in the same way
that everyone else did.

00:02:21.440 --> 00:02:24.680
That the way that I
constantly criticised myself,

00:02:25.000 --> 00:02:26.560
pushed myself to excel

00:02:26.640 --> 00:02:29.600
and was cautious about
celebrating achievements

00:02:29.920 --> 00:02:31.000
wasn't the norm.

00:02:32.320 --> 00:02:33.600
Actually that's not quite right -

00:02:34.400 --> 00:02:36.800
it wasn't the norm
for my white friends

00:02:37.120 --> 00:02:39.680
but it was normal
for the women in my family.

00:02:39.880 --> 00:02:42.280
They struggled to be
what was expected of them

00:02:43.240 --> 00:02:47.160
but instead of rejecting the
stereotype, they perpetuated it -

00:02:47.880 --> 00:02:50.600
a survival mechanism
which allowed them to

00:02:50.680 --> 00:02:55.600
integrate and build communities in a
hostile and unwelcoming environment.

00:02:57.040 --> 00:02:58.480
But at what cost?

00:02:58.560 --> 00:03:02.720
I would see glimpses of
their pain and anger in private

00:03:02.800 --> 00:03:04.480
but they would quickly hide it away.

00:03:04.720 --> 00:03:06.760
From an early age I was taught

00:03:06.840 --> 00:03:08.960
that you should never
let anyone see your pain

00:03:09.320 --> 00:03:11.640
because they would
use it against you.

00:03:11.920 --> 00:03:14.400
There was a sense
that life was a battle.

00:03:14.480 --> 00:03:17.960
To win you had to work
twice as hard as everyone else

00:03:18.040 --> 00:03:22.040
for half as much reward
and keep smiling and laughing

00:03:22.120 --> 00:03:22.960
but not too much.

00:03:23.040 --> 00:03:26.640
If you were too happy then
someone would take it away from you.

00:03:27.280 --> 00:03:28.840
That's a lot, right?

00:03:29.320 --> 00:03:31.200
Where does that mindset come from?

00:03:32.200 --> 00:03:35.440
And how does a black woman
navigate her way through it all?

00:03:35.920 --> 00:03:39.400
Luckily, there are mental health
organisations out there

00:03:39.480 --> 00:03:43.640
that are specifically designed to
support the black British community.

00:03:44.280 --> 00:03:46.840
I spoke to Jacqui Dyer
and Natalie Creary

00:03:46.920 --> 00:03:49.240
from Black Thrive
to discuss their work.

00:03:50.240 --> 00:03:52.920
So where do you think
the stereotype of the

00:03:53.000 --> 00:03:55.160
strong independent black
woman comes from?

00:03:55.800 --> 00:03:58.520
I think it comes all the way back
from slavery to be quite frank.

00:03:58.800 --> 00:04:01.400
These are some of the
stories that went round

00:04:01.480 --> 00:04:04.960
in order to help to enslave
our black population.

00:04:05.040 --> 00:04:06.320
The black woman is expected

00:04:06.400 --> 00:04:08.920
to carry the weight of the world
on their shoulder

00:04:09.560 --> 00:04:14.200
and to provide the support
and the care for others

00:04:14.480 --> 00:04:17.440
when actually black women
also need to be cared for -

00:04:17.520 --> 00:04:18.800
they need to be nurtured.

00:04:19.200 --> 00:04:22.840
Where else can black British women
receive help and support?

00:04:23.240 --> 00:04:27.480
Take some steps and communicate
what is going on inside you

00:04:27.560 --> 00:04:30.880
that you actually don't need
to be bearing on your own.

00:04:30.960 --> 00:04:35.120
To be a strong black woman is also
to be a vulnerable black woman.

00:04:35.400 --> 00:04:40.520
I'd say find somebody who you
can trust, who you can talk with.

00:04:40.720 --> 00:04:44.000
It's going to see your GP,
reaching out to your social networks.

00:04:44.080 --> 00:04:46.800
We need to be able
to let our guard down

00:04:46.880 --> 00:04:49.360
and to say,
"Actually I'm feeling vulnerable."

00:04:49.440 --> 00:04:50.880
And, "I need help."

00:04:51.360 --> 00:04:54.120
The strong, independent
black woman trope

00:04:54.480 --> 00:04:56.840
was a much-needed survival mechanism,

00:04:58.040 --> 00:05:01.040
but for second and
third-generation black women

00:05:01.520 --> 00:05:04.080
it's a mechanism
that has served its purpose

00:05:04.840 --> 00:05:08.840
and has the potential to become
a self-destructive identity

00:05:09.120 --> 00:05:11.400
that can actually do
more harm than good.

00:05:11.800 --> 00:05:15.080
I'm so grateful for the sacrifices
made by my elders

00:05:15.600 --> 00:05:17.600
and the hardships they had to endure.

00:05:19.080 --> 00:05:21.720
Their strength will always
lie within me

00:05:22.760 --> 00:05:26.120
which means I can allow myself
to be more than just strong.

00:05:27.560 --> 00:05:30.960
Out of respect for them,
I'm learning to show

00:05:31.040 --> 00:05:33.840
the sides of myself
that they had to keep hidden away.

00:05:35.440 --> 00:05:37.320
It's OK not to be OK.

00:05:37.920 --> 00:05:41.440
It takes courage to speak honestly
about how you're feeling.

00:05:42.920 --> 00:05:46.480
I can be strong
and accept my weaknesses.

00:05:47.600 --> 00:05:50.160
Independent and vulnerable.

00:05:51.200 --> 00:05:55.800
Move away from the stereotype
and embrace my true self.

